  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Box Is the Promise",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Box Is the Promise\"**\n\nSkipQ is a warm, plain-spoken phone app for a coffee locker. The whole system exists to answer two questions at a glance: which door holds your coffee, and until when. The ground is Foam, a café-paper off-white; content sits on white Surface cards edged with a 1 px Line border; actions are Coffee; anything that is *yours* (your chosen chip, your slot, your box, your board row, your gift ticket) turns Vanilla. Espresso is reserved for the machine itself and for the tester's instruments: the locker frame, the Unlock button, toasts, the Demo pill and Demo panel.\n\nDensity is phone-native and calm: one column inside a 20 px gutter, 24 px between blocks, cards that hold one thing each. Three families split the work cleanly. Instrument Serif speaks in greetings, titles and the ready moment. DM Sans runs the interface. DM Mono carries every number a student has to read against a clock: times, box numbers, codes, counters, prices. Icons are drawn line icons (24 px grid, 1.75 stroke, round caps), never glyphs or emoji.\n\nThe world refuses the coffee-app defaults it was briefed against: no product-photo menu, no loyalty-card gamification, no fake urgency. Limits are honest and neutral, so nothing about a limit is ever red.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Foam ground, white bordered cards, Coffee actions, Vanilla for \"yours\", Espresso for the machine and the demo tooling.",
      "The locker plate: the box number is always a Vanilla door plate with a mono numeral.",
      "The machine is always drawn as an Espresso frame of 6 x 5 doors with handles, at full size or as a 30-door strip.",
      "Serif only at 24 px and above, for greetings, titles and the ready moment.",
      "Status is always icon plus word plus colour.",
      "Mostly flat; a shadow means \"floating\" or \"this one is live\"."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Tokens-Only Rule",
        "body": "Every colour comes from `tokens.css`. App-level derivatives in `base.css` are `color-mix()` of tokens, never a new hex. The client's hand-off file is binding.",
